Feature: Compute metric columns by executing SQL queries from the metric_master table

  Background:
    Given the Unity Catalog information:
      | catalog       | schema          |
      | purgo_databricks | purgo_playground |
    And the following tables exist:
      | table_name                |
      | metric_config             |
      | metric_master             |
      | medical_inquiry_data      |
      | mska                      |

  Scenario Outline: Execute SQL queries from the metric_master table in the correct order
    Given the metric_config table contains the following data:
      | metric_id | active_indicator | source_table | primary_key | target_table |
      | <metric_id> | <active_indicator> | <source_table> | <primary_key> | <target_table> |
    And the metric_master table contains the following data:
      | metric_template_name | sql_query | view_name | dependency |
      | <metric_template_name> | <sql_query> | <view_name> | <dependency> |
    When the PySpark script is executed
    Then the SQL queries are executed in the correct order based on the dependency column
    And the results are loaded into the target tables

    Examples:
      | metric_id | active_indicator | source_table | primary_key | target_table | metric_template_name | sql_query | view_name | dependency |
      | metric1   | Y                | medical_inquiry_data | id          | mska         | metric1_template    | SELECT * FROM medical_inquiry_data | null      | 1          |
      | metric2   | Y                | mska                | id          | mska         | metric2_template    | SELECT * FROM mska | null      | 2          |

  Scenario: Handle cases where the view_name column is null
    Given the metric_config table contains the following data:
      | metric_id | active_indicator | source_table | primary_key | target_table |
      | metric1   | Y                | medical_inquiry_data | id          | mska         |
    And the metric_master table contains the following data:
      | metric_template_name | sql_query | view_name | dependency |
      | metric1_template    | SELECT * FROM medical_inquiry_data | null      | 1          |
    When the PySpark script is executed
    Then the results are loaded directly into the target table

  Scenario: Handle errors that occur during execution
    Given the metric_config table contains the following data:
      | metric_id | active_indicator | source_table | primary_key | target_table |
      | metric1   | Y                | medical_inquiry_data | id          | mska         |
    And the metric_master table contains the following data:
      | metric_template_name | sql_query | view_name | dependency |
      | metric1_template    | SELECT * FROM non_existent_table | null      | 1          |
    When the PySpark script is executed
    Then an error is logged indicating that the table does not exist

  Scenario: Handle circular dependencies
    Given the metric_config table contains the following data:
      | metric_id | active_indicator | source_table | primary_key | target_table |
      | metric1   | Y                | medical_inquiry_data | id          | mska         |
      | metric2   | Y                | mska                | id          | mska         |
    And the metric_master table contains the following data:
      | metric_template_name | sql_query | view_name | dependency |
      | metric1_template    | SELECT * FROM medical_inquiry_data | null      | 1          |
      | metric2_template    | SELECT * FROM mska | null      | 1          |
    When the PySpark script is executed
    Then an error is logged indicating a circular dependency

  Scenario: Handle missing dependencies
    Given the metric_config table contains the following data:
      | metric_id | active_indicator | source_table | primary_key | target_table |
      | metric1   | Y                | medical_inquiry_data | id          | mska         |
    And the metric_master table contains the following data:
      | metric_template_name | sql_query | view_name | dependency |
      | metric1_template    | SELECT * FROM medical_inquiry_data | null      | 2          |
    When the PySpark script is executed
    Then an error is logged indicating a missing dependency
